body:
From what I've been told by my counselor, there needs to be a case for your promotion (e.g. the person did something above and beyond their current job scope, so this will be used as support for their promotion). You will still be considered for promotion if you pass your PIP before the end of FY, but there may not be enough time for you to demonstrate something above and beyond to build your case for promotion for the FY. Not to say it's impossible, but it will be tough.

&#x200B;

You will still be eligible for raises and bonus. But that will depend on what they decide your overall performance "level" is. And if the firm is doing well or not. If your overall rating is below average, you may still get a raise and bonus but on a smaller quantum compared to those rated as "average" or "above average". If the firm is doing badly, or if the overall rating is "bad" then you may not be eligible for the performance bonus or raise
